Marine heatwaves are conventionally detected from daily sea-surface-temperature (SST) products, an approach that obscures the threshold crossings, interruptions and persistence that occur within the diurnal cycle. We present CUDAHeat, a GPU-native Python framework for detecting and characterising marine heatwaves in contiguous hourly SST records. The framework constructs a seasonally and diurnally resolved reference state by pooling same-clock-hour observations in a calendar window, estimating a percentile threshold and applying same-hour circular smoothing. It then distinguishes raw threshold exceedance from event continuity: short bounded interruptions may be filled, candidate intervals are filtered by a minimum duration, and neighbouring qualifying intervals may be joined according to explicitly reported temporal rules.

CUDAHeat preserves the complete temporal record of every spatial point and batches only the spatial domain, thereby avoiding changes in event definition at computational boundaries. Detection returns a sparse event catalogue with timestamp-resolved intervals, while optional lazy reconstruction provides dense event identifiers only when required. A versioned YAML enrichment system evaluates event-aligned metrics, including threshold occupancy, cumulative exceedance, diurnal amplification and nocturnal retention. These descriptors represent each event as a transparent multivariate feature vector suitable for subsequent normalized-scalar analysis, principal component analysis and scatterplot-based exploration without imposing a fixed classification.

By separating climatology construction, temporal event rules and enrichment provenance, CUDAHeat provides a reproducible basis for analysing the sub-daily morphology of marine thermal extremes. The framework is intended to support scientifically explicit comparison of hourly events across SST products, regions and applications while retaining the full temporal detail needed to investigate diurnal structure and thermal persistence.
